  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cloudy the rest of the day, with areas of fog on \nThursday morning.\n\nA weak shortwave disturbance brings intermittent overcast skies to \nthe Mid-Atlantic today, with increased low-level moisture lending \nto a brief period of morning fog and low clouds on Thursday \nmorning. Warm advection persists through the afternoon as a weak \nwarm front lifts north along with persistent onshore flow. This will \nallow for an increase in dewpoints/moisture on Thursday, before the \ndisturbance gradually transitions offshore by Friday morning. \n\nCombined with overall light winds, areas of fog could settle in by \nearly Thursday morning, with some areas seeing reduced visibility \nbelow 5 miles at times. Fog will likely be concentrated along \nand east of the I-95 corridor through 14Z, with areas further west \nlikely seeing overall patchier fog. As the shortwave disturbance \ntransitions offshore, clouds clear in the afternoon, with warm \nafternoon temperatures ranging in the low-to-mid 70s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Above normal temperatures through the end of \nthe week, with increasing clouds heading into the weekend. \n\nAs the midweek shortwave disturbance transitions offshore, \nanother brief period of upper-level ridging builds over the Mid-\nAtlantic between Thursday afternoon and Friday. Winds could gust up \nto 15-20 knots throughout the day on Friday as temperatures range \nwell above average with afternoon highs in the mid-70s. \n\nAn incoming cold front could bring overnight temperatures back down \ninto the 40s between Friday & Saturday across the metros, with temps \npotentially in  the upper 30s over the mountains. Saturday will see \nincreasing cloud cover throughout the day as this front moves \nthrough the region, bringing temperatures back down to more \nseasonable daytime ranges in the 60s. No precipitation is \nanticipated for most of the weekend as this front slowly begins to \ntrack through into early next week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...On-and-off rain showers possible Sunday night \nthrough Tuesday.\n\nHigh pressure should keep most, if not all, of the region dry \nthrough the heart of the day Sunday. Rain chances could arrive with \nan approaching low pressure system Sunday night and linger through \nTuesday. It is too early to tell how much in coverage these showers \nwill be each period, but some areas may not see any showers at all. \nAll in all, rain amounts could be light. Temperatures will be \nseasonable Sunday, but then inch their way upwards Monday and \nTuesday as a southerly to southwesterly wind brings in slightly \nmilder air. Highs Sunday in the middle 60s for most locations, then \nclimb into the upper 60s to near 70 for both Monday and Tuesday.",
